    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7842",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/83e7c4533710e42e16cbe883188f619321914815d905344547bd6c81d253a3ac.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-2$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,-2y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(2,1),$ $(3,1),$ $(3,3),$  $(1,3),$ $(1,2),$  and $(2,2),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-4,-2) \\\\ (3,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-6,-2) \\\\ (3,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-6,-6) \\\\ (1,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-2,-6) \\\\ (1,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4) \\\\ (2,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-4,-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/a3ce2ccd16db3e9013947b475bbca5fec1d34ff5e10414eff426e8389271c219.png)",
